solution:
Walk the string once. At each position, find where the current run ends, write its length and its digit, and jump to the next run.

```js
function lookAndSay(s) {
  let out = '';
  let i = 0;
  while (i < s.length) {
    let j = i;
    while (j < s.length && s[j] === s[i]) j++;
    out += String(j - i) + s[i];
    i = j;
  }
  return out;
}
```

The inner loop advances `j` while the digit stays the same, so `j - i` is the run's length. `String(j - i)` writes the full count, which handles runs of ten or more; code that stores the count as a single character breaks there. An empty string skips the loop and returns `''`.

Starting from `"1"` and applying this again and again gives the look-and-say sequence: 1, 11, 21, 1211, 111221, 312211, … Curiously, no digit larger than 3 ever appears in it.
